# Narrow false-positive allowlist for scripts/secret-scan.sh (one ERE per line).
#
# Fake values in internal/cli/doctor_shell_alias_test.go: the test asserts that
# an alias VALUE never reaches a doctor Detail, so the fixture has to look like
# a secret. They are not credentials. The key name is spelled with a bracket
# expression so this line does not match the scanner's own pattern.
api_ke[y]=SECRET(123|456)
# The first spelling of the rule above is still in this branch's history
# (commit 5a91cff) and the range scan reads history, so allow that one line.
SECRET[(]123[|]456[)]
# Hygiene sentences in docs/reports/{test,verify}-2026-09-19-codex-agmsg-
# writable-root.md name several secret-key pattern names in backticks to
# assert they are ABSENT from fixtures, so naming the pattern trips the
# scanner even though no value follows it. Requires the closing backtick
# immediately after "=" (nothing in between), so a real secret pasted into
# a backtick-quoted span (which would have a value between "=" and the
# closing backtick) still matches the scanner, not this rule. Spelled with
# a bracket expression for the same self-match reason as the entry above.
`api_ke[y]=`
# The first spelling of that comment (commit 90dc4be) quoted the flagged
# sentences verbatim, without breaking the pattern names, and is still in
# this branch's history — so allow that spelling too. Same bracket-
# expression trick, applied to the other keyword this rule's own history
# happens to repeat.
`passwor[d]=`
